ryan giggs has spent most of his life in  the spotlight he came to court to hear a  prosecutor describe his football skills  as abundant and a thing of beauty but  off the pitch in his personal life a  character that was much uglier and more  sinister  what they described as a toxic  relationship with his girlfriend kate  greville culminated in an argument at  their home in november 2020 as she tried  to end the relationship the prosecutions  say he deliberately head-butted her and  elbowed her sister in the jaw  peter wright qc said mr giggs was  obsessive controlling and coercive  this was a private life that involved a  litany of abuse he said both physical  and psychological of a woman he  professed to love a woman who in reality  was if the evidence is to be believed  treated in a way that cannot be excused  or overlooked by either an adoring  public or the law this is a story of  control and coercion of a woman who  thought she was loved and respected  sadly the reality was very different  the prosecution said kate greville  described various events over the years  where mr giggs would harass her  threatening to send photographs of a  personal nature to friends and  colleagues throwing her out of their  home there were acts of violence and  volatility mr wright said followed by  kindness and contrition calculated to  gaslight her into doubting herself and  her instincts  ryan giggs is one of the most decorated  players in football history 13 premier  league titles in more than 20 years with  manchester united and until november  2020 the manager of the welsh national  team  the trial was told it may hear from his  former united manager sir alex ferguson  before court ended for the day the  barrister for mr giggs told the jury he  and kate greville had squabbled like  teenagers but that he'd never used  unlawful violence the allegations he  said were based on distortion  exaggeration and lies any contact  between their heads he said was minor  and accidental  brian giggs denies the charges against  him and the trial continues greg milam  sky news manchester
 

